{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Moses hurried.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this prostration was an acknowledgment for the attribute of grace and mercy that He had made known. And far be it to believe a thing of empty minds, which R. Avraham brought and explained — that he hurried lest He prolong further and say the remaining fifty, for the Holy One, blessed be He, would not have interrupted His word, heaven forbid; and behold, one does not do thus even for a king of flesh and blood, as he, of blessed memory, objected against them." "<b>But</b> the explanation of the verse is that after the Holy One, blessed be He, had completed His words, Moses hurried with great alacrity and prostrated himself toward the Glory that passed before him — like one who receives a gift from the king's palace and prostrates himself and gives Him acknowledgment for what He gave him when he goes forth from before Him." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And Moses hurried' — what did he see? R. Chanina ben Gamliel says: he saw 'long-suffering (ארך אפים).' The Rabbis say: he saw 'truth (אמת).' Thus they expounded in the Midrash Tehillim. And the explanation of this is as it is plainly stated: for according to R. Chanina's view, Moses gave acknowledgment for 'long-suffering,' for it is an attribute that all Israel will need in every generation — that the Holy One, blessed be He, will be long-suffering toward them and not put them to shame for their iniquity. And according to the Rabbis' view, he gave acknowledgment for the attribute of truth, which is most abundant in pardon, to have mercy upon them completely and not remember their iniquity — for 'long-suffering' is a somewhat harsh attribute compared to 'long-suffering toward them,' and it is applied to Him, as it were." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'he saw long-suffering' — 'he saw truth.' R. Chanina and the Rabbis appear to disagree but they do not disagree, for 'long-suffering' is the force drawn from Gevurah, and it is the decisive one, and this is the view of the Rabbis who said 'he saw truth' — and they did not change the formulation except to inform us whether the attributes are enumerated from above to below or from below to above. R. Chanina counted from below to above and concluded with 'long-suffering' as the decisive one, and therefore he said 'long-suffering'; and the Rabbis counted from above to below and concluded with 'and truth' as the decisive one, and therefore they said 'he saw truth.'" "<b>And further</b> there in the Midrash Tehillim: on which of them did he prostrate himself? R. Eliezer ben Yaakov says: on 'long-suffering.' The Rabbis say: on the attribute of mercy. And what emerges for us from this is that all is one, and the decision and the prostration were toward truth, and this is clear."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:59.287550608Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}